{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790361424,"nonce":"gwdip/UEyQQ5gDfrpqUbp6Rif9s=","message":"The fiber, effect and async composition plan (docs/FIBER_EFFECT_ASYNC_COMPOSITION.md, replaced by docs/ASYNC_EFFECTS_FIBERS.md) ended with a single scheduler, which belongs with this issue. Its scope beyond M:N fibers:\n\n1. Fibers, async tasks and resumable-effect continuations become variants of one task type that suspends through one envelope. Today there are three mechanisms: krio-fiber stacks resumed by `krio_fiber_resume`, krio state machines polled by `drive_until` and `host_futures::resolve_future`, and effect state machines re-polled by `__zyntax_effect_resume`.\n2. The thread-locals that stand in for per-task storage move into the task: `HANDLER_STACK` with its per-fiber and per-task segments (`HANDLER_SEGMENTS`, `TASK_HANDLER_SEGMENTS`), `FUTURE_TABLE`, `TIMER_QUEUE`, `SM_COMPLETIONS`, `HANDLER_TO_PERFORMER`, `CURRENT_TASK_ID`, and the fiber backend's `ABORT_PAYLOAD`, `ERROR_MAP`, `TASK_FIBERS`.\n3. A `spawn` primitive returning a handle, with `select` and `race` across fibers and tasks, and fair multiplexing of spawned fibers.\n\nIt also carried an invariant this work has to settle: `krio_fiber_resume` accepts a call from any thread. Only the host handle `ZyntaxFiber` checks the creator thread; the C ABI and `KrioFiberBackend` do not. Until fibers may move, resumption should be checked against the thread that made the fiber.","files":null}]}